Warm starting: scale the cached contact impulses themselves by the step ratio,
not only what is applied. With only the applied impulse scaled, the solver could
take back just the unscaled amount, so a step longer than the one before left
the excess in as a kick (a landed piece thrown up the screen after a slow frame).

--- a/cpArbiter.c
+++ b/cpArbiter.c
@@ -447,8 +447,11 @@
 	
 	for(int i=0; i<arb->count; i++){
 		struct cpContact *con = &arb->contacts[i];
+		// Scale the totals too, as Box2D does, or a longer step leaves the excess in as a kick.
+		con->jnAcc *= dt_coef;
+		con->jtAcc *= dt_coef;
 		cpVect j = cpvrotate(n, cpv(con->jnAcc, con->jtAcc));
-		apply_impulses(a, b, con->r1, con->r2, cpvmult(j, dt_coef));
+		apply_impulses(a, b, con->r1, con->r2, j);
 	}
 }
 
